  "file": "src/git/command.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent leaves a populated nested repository with a malicious filter and changes the parent\u2019s submodule mapping. When the factory prepares a merge, it executes that filter outside the agent\u2019s confinement.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-539.DGzhM5/probe.ts\"",
    "output": "Git 2.54.0; fixtures used recorded linked worktrees and independent nested Git directories.\nF1 empty prepareMerge: []\nF1 empty filter marker: true\nF1 include-only prepareMerge: []\nF1 include-only filter marker: true\nF1 remapped prepareMerge: []\nF1 remapped filter marker: true\nEach trace showed: git stash create; cd nested; ... GIT_DIR=.git git status --porcelain=2 -uno; execution of the configured touch-marker clean filter."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD chooses the existing worktree file with `let source = \"--file=.gitmodules\"` and falls back to index/HEAD only when that file is absent. It queries `--no-includes ... ^submodule\\\\..*\\\\.path$` and emits only name-specific overrides: `--config-env=${key.slice(0, -4)}ignore=LIMITLESS_GIT_SUBMODULE_IGNORE`. All three mapping variants allowed the nested filter to execute. Base also lacked protection for merge\u2019s internal dirty checks, so this is an unmet explicit requirement to inspect other worktree commands for the same recursion, rather than a newly introduced escape."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent creates a nested repository inside its checkout. The repository is committed with a .gitmodules entry, and the nested repository's own config defines a clean filter. The agent then empties the .gitmodules file in the working tree. When the daemon next merges the base branch in, git's internal dirty check enters the nested repository and runs the agent's filter outside the sandbox.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test $TMPDIR/adj539/repro.test.ts (throwaway test: commit a populated gitlink plus .gitmodules with ignore=none, advance the base, set filter.attack.clean in nested/.git/config with '* filter=attack' in its info/attributes, touch the file's mtime, write an empty worktree .gitmodules, call prepareMerge)",
    "output": "emptied marker ran: true err: \n(control: with no .gitmodules ever committed, marker ran: false)"
   },
   "evidence": "In command.ts:131-132 (head), merge, rebase and commit get `submoduleFlags`. That function reads `--file=.gitmodules` whenever the worktree file exists (lines 159-160) and emits `--config-env=submodule.<name>.ignore=...` only for names listed there. An empty or edited worktree file therefore yields no overrides, while git's internal stash-create/merge dirty check still inspects the populated gitlink. Base had no protection on this path at all. The request asks for every work-tree call in src/git, including checkout steps, to be checked for this recursion, and the head's new guard on merges can be bypassed."
  }
 },
